-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_overdispersion_in_count_models_76b7c45e_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A negative-binomial conditional claim count has mean $\\mu=2$ and size $r=4$. Its variance is $\\mu+\\mu^2/r$. What is that variance?","options":["$2$.","$1$.","$2.5$.","$3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a2a7d4723b475f55713d882","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_overdispersion_in_count_models_76b7c45e_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For mean $\\mu>0$ and quasi factor $\\phi=3/2$, someone proposes a literal count model $Y=\\phi Z$ with $Z\\sim\\operatorname{Pois}(\\mu/\\phi)$. It matches mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\phi\\mu$. What hidden problem remains?","options":["It assigns positive probability to a fractional count when Z equals one.","Its matched variance proves it is the intended full quasi-Poisson law.","Its support stays integer-valued because the unscaled variable is a count.","Its mean cannot be mu because scaling never changes Poisson means."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a7651aa85246a2ea8168a2ef","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_overdispersion_in_count_models_76b7c45e_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Two equally common observed risk groups have conditional Poisson counts with means one and five. The pooled mean is three and pooled variance seven. Does this imply conditional overdispersion after group is included as a predictor?","options":["Yes; pooled variance seven exceeds pooled mean three.","No; each group still has conditional variance equal to its own mean.","Yes; different group means violate every Poisson conditional model.","No; pooled variance must be replaced by the squared pooled mean nine."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db31879b06a635895d80a95c","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}